  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe current algorithm uses standard optimization techniques with multiple restarts, but we can significantly improve performance by incorporating problem-specific knowledge (symmetry) and using a more efficient, scalable differentiation method. The key bottleneck is the high dimensionality (up to 200 points) and numerical instability in gradient computations. \n\nWe propose:\n1. Symmetric half-representation: Reduce problem dimension by 2-4x using symmetric functions (h(x) = h(2-x))\n2. Second-order differentiation with automatic differentiation (AD) via `autograd` to replace numerical gradients\n3. Parallel coordinate optimization with synchronized parent fusions\n4. Polynomial interpolation-based restart strategy\n\nThe plan systematically balances global search and local refinement using:\n- Symmetry-invariant objective \n- Bound-preserving integral normalization\n- Piecewise repair procedures\n- Adaptive restart framework\n\nMathematical specification:\nVariables:|   \nh(x) \u2208 [0,1] for x \u2208 [0,2]\nIntegral constraint: \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\nObjective: minimize C = max_k \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx dx\n\nSymmetric representation:\nDefine variables x \u2208 R^{floor(n/2)} (for n=70: x\u2080\u2085\u2080, then n=140: x\u2080\u2087\u2080)\nConstraint: h_total = vcat(x, reverse(x)) for even n\n\nObjective evaluation:\nc = convolve1d(h_total, 1-h_total, mode='full') * (2/n)\nC = max(c)\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initial configuration\n- If parent exists: estimate dB and normalize\n- Construct symmetric sample vectors\n\n2. Core components:\n- Symmetric build: x_to_h(x) = vcat(x, x[::-1])\n- Integral normalization: sum(h) = n/4.0\n- Second-derivative safe AD via autograd\n\n3. Optimization loop:\n- Structured restarts: polynomial interpolation, frequency padding\n- Distributed gradient-based updates\n- Synchronized synchronous updates\n\n4. Adaptive phases:\n- Exploration phase (200 generations)\n- Exploitation phase (refinement)\n- Progressive dimension reduction\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Base resolution: 70, 100, 140\n- Polynomial degrees: [1,2,3] determined by signal \n- Penalty scaling: 10^{-k} response surfaces\n\nBudget and robustness:\n- Wall-clock time: 1100s < 9000ms\n- Memory: ~1000 floats per thread\n- Failure recovery: fallback polynomial interpolation\n\nValidation and interfaces:\n- Check finite values\n- Periodic feasibility repair\n- Symmetry enforcement\n- Smooth objective scaling",
